WEBVTT

NOTE
Automatic English captions. Generated locally and checked with additional audio decodes; not fully audited by listening.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.320
Out of the box, an agent only knows what
its model was trained on. Nothing about

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:10.740
your company, your docs, or your product.
A knowledge base fixes that. You give it

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.860
your documents, and it becomes a
searchable memory your workflows can pull

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:18.240
from, so when an agent needs an answer,
it's grounded in your material instead of

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:23.120
guessing. And when you use knowledge bases
as a component in your workflows, you can

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:27.720
build AI systems that actually understand
your business. Let me show you how it

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:33.020
works. Your company's knowledge is
scattered across docs, wikis, PDFs,

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:38.620
spreadsheets, and inboxes. The naive fix
is to stuff it all into the prompt, but

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:42.800
cram in everything and the model drowns.
Too much context and the answer falls

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.960
apart. The fix is a knowledge base. You
collect those scattered sources into one

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:52.920
place, and Sim makes everything inside it
searchable. A knowledge base is a

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:58.640
resource. It lives in your workspace right
alongside your workflows. And you can have

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.520
more than one. Separate bases for
engineering, sales, support. So each

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.820
workflow searches only the knowledge it
should. You probably already have

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:10.680
knowledge you'd like your workflows to
use. Here, a customer asks about the

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.100
refund policy, and the question reaches
the knowledge block.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:19.600
Inside a workflow, the knowledge block
points at one of those bases. This one

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.540
holds your product docs, 17 of them.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:28.960
Inside a knowledge base, the key unit of
information is the document. A document

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:33.760
gets processed by Sim into smaller pieces
called chunks. And it's the chunks that

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:38.360
get searched, not whole files. When a
question comes in, it's matched against

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:43.280
every chunk by meaning. And the closest
few come back. The pieces most likely to

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:44.280
hold the answer.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:51.860
Those chunks get inserted into the model's
context. So before the agent writes a

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:56.440
single word, it's already looking at your
actual policy. And the answer it derives

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:57.920
is grounded in your documents.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:05.720
And every search leaves a record. The
question, the chunks it pulled, and the

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:11.320
answer it produced. You can always see why
it said what it said. There's no magic in

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:15.360
any of this. It's your documents made
searchable and brought to the model at the

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:16.380
moment it needs them.
